Usual apologies if these have been suggested before. Most of them are
a little spoily, since they allude to things already in the game.

1- I want a chainsaw! I'm sure this has been suggested before (it
might even be in slashem already) and I'm aware that it's stretching
the fantasy setting a little (even though we already have cameras,
credit cards and land mines) but it would be such a very very joyfully
violent thing to own. It should be an incredibly dangerous two-handed
weapon with extreme maim/ decapitation/ destroy door capabilities, but
balanced by the fact that it is very heavy, very loud (wakes/ annoys
monsters) and requires fuel. Like a lamp you'd switch it on and off,
and once the fuel runs out it's not very effective as a weapon. A few
cans of petrol might be lying about so you can refuel but you'd better
hope you don't get hit by any flame weapons while carrying (or
standing anywhere near) one. If you use a chainsaw without appropriate
eye protection (goggles, lenses, visored helmet) you risk getting
blinded by fragments of debris.
A less powerful, lightweight hedgetrimmer weapon could also be
available.

2-Using axes (and chainsaws, see above) on trees. I've never tried
axing a tree, so someone stop me if this is already in the game. You
ought to be able to cut down trees, with the following effects:
-All occupants/ contents of the tree are released, as per kicking.
-Unless skilled in axe (chainsaw), you risk having the tree fall on
your head. Ouch. You should wear a hard hat.
-Elves suffer loss of stats/ loss of alignment (in the case of
peaceful elves) / wrath of the Gods / maybe even the ball & chain.
-Orcs (dwarves too?) Get good alignment/ goodwill of gods/ improved
wisdom.
-Once felled, the tree trunk is represented by a stack of
quarterstaffs and/or crude wooden clubs and/or wood golem(s).
-Guards (in gnometown and similar) get upset at this act of wanton
vandalism.
-Any wood nymphs or elves on the level get pissed off at you.
-Like pushing boulders, tree-felling is hard physical exercise and
builds you up.

3- An orchard/ forest level. Lots of trees, lots of elvish guardians
to protect them, lots of giant bugs, lots of wood nymphs. Maybe a
stream/ pond and an altar to some woodland God. Not sure why, but then
again why not?

4-After the above three suggestions, I just have to introduce the idea
of a Lumberjack character class. Open to dwarves and humans, they
start with goggles, an axe/ chainsaw, some food rations and a small
dog. High strength, constitution, and a strange compulsion to
cross-dress. Lots and lots of other Monty Python gags, which I'll
leave for all of you to fill in.

4- I understand that in slashem you get rooms with sinks and toilets.
Surely these rooms should also come equipped with a towel on a towel
rail and a roll of toilet paper. The towel rail could be pulled off
the wall and used as a crude weapon. The toilet paper could be used in
conjuction with a magic marker for... something. Not sure what, but I
don't think it should be just a blank scroll. Perhaps it's a blank
scroll that can only be used to write stinking cloud. Maybe you could
also use it as a blindfold.

5- Was thinking about nethack alchemy the other day and the original
meaning of alchemy. Perhaps destroying sinks (and toilets, where
available) could reveal fragments of lead plumbing, which could be
polymorphed (if you're inclined to waste your precious wand/ potion of
polymorph this way) into a meagre stash of gold pieces. Someone
mentioned implementing Fools Gold a while back, maybe this could be
incorporated into that.

6- Thinking about lead plumbing/ lead piping got me thinking about
Cleudo. Nethack+Cleudo... Professor Plum wields a candlestick!
Professor Plum hits! Professor Plum hits! ... there's an idea for a
Sobokan-style subgame in there somewhere, but I'll be damned if I can
work out the details.

7- I thought of a neat new character class, although I've no idea what
to call them: They'd have low combat and spellcasting abilities, but
their strength is that they can talk to animals. Kind of a St Francis
of asissi/ Doctor Dolittle character.
-Start with some ?of Taming, a pile of tripe rations/ fruit&veg, and a
whole zoofull of pets.
-They'd get a bonus casting "charm monster" spells (although the have
to find the spellbook for themselves)
-Most "wild animal" monsters (f,d,Y,S,u,q,a others I can't think of)
would be generated peaceful.
-They'd get penalised for eating meat or killing *any* of the above
animal types.
-They'd also be allowed to use rings of conflict without animal pets
being affected: sentient pets would still turn on their master.

8- Another new class: Bard. 
-Auto-identifies any musical instruments, and very good in their use.
-starts off with a random selection of harps, horns, whistles,
whatever.
-Below average combat/ magic ailities.
-Not open to orcs. 
-I think there should be some kind of special treatment when
confronted with courtly characters (elvenking, gnome lord etc).
Perhaps those monsters are generated peaceful, and bards can play
music to extract coins/ food from them. Bards sound badly
underpowered, but that would just make them a "challenge class" for
experienced players.

9- Ripping spellbooks: If you get really desperate you should be able
to tear a spellbook into scrolls of that spell. For example, I might
have a spellbook of teleport and know the spell, but be unable to cast
it because a> I'm just a level 2 barbarian sword-monkey or b> I'm a
bitchin' warlock but happen to have run out of magic-juice on this
occasion. In this case I could sacrifice the spellbook by tearing the
pages out to gain a random number (maybe d4-1, modified by BUC of
spellbook) of teleport scrolls. Same for any other spellbooks that
have corresponding scrolls (taming, blank, remove curse, ID...)
Defiling a spellbook in this way should also risk cursing the
resultant scrolls (if any) and/or producing scrolls of punishment. Oh,
and spellcaster/ intellectual types should lose wisdom and luck points
for doing this, except maybe in the case of blank spellbooks.

